      children: "The Saga pattern, introduced by Hector Garcia-Molina and Kenneth Salem in 1987, provides a formal framework for managing long-lived transactions in distributed systems using compensating actions. A saga breaks a distributed transaction into a sequence of local transactions, each performed by a single service. Each local transaction updates its own database and publishes an event or message to trigger the next step. If any step fails, the saga executes compensating transactions to undo the work of preceding steps that succeeded."
